after a bloody battle I put it into it  all in the catacombs beneath the city  the penguin two-face at lady Arkham were  the first real challenge I faced as the  Batman but they would not be the last  actions have consequences  and in season one of Telltale's Batman  series Bruce Wayne learned this truths  the hard way when his father Thomas  Wayne was revealed to be one of the most  powerful criminals in Gotham history  while the events of season 1 mostly  wrapped up neatly one very important  thread was left laying bare at one point  in order to escape incarceration at  Arkham Asylum Bruce Wayne briefly allied  himself with John Doe a green haired  pale faced lunatic with a chilling  air-to-air grin and as we all know  actions have consequences when I get out  of here you're gonna owe me a favor okay  just one but the enigma season 2 episode  1 of tale tells Batman series approaches  this loose end with a deft touch  allowing the threat of John Doe to  linger in the background ever present  but simmering instead it pivots to a  compelling take on the Riddler who is  excellently voiced by Robert Atkin  Downes  although Riddler is primarily used as  the setup for a much bigger threat  telltale still does justice to the  character with a gripping self-contained  story telltale has made a few changes to  Edward nygma to present him in a way  that while not completely unique puts a  different spin on him in this universe  Riddler existed before Bruce Wayne  became Batman but has been missing for  some time his return to Gotham is not  only an ominous sign of things to come  but the spark that ignites a number of  fights for Batman and Bruce Wayne to  fight in typical Riddler fashion he  immediately sets about challenging the  intellect of Gotham so-called Savior the  initial confrontation is a brutal Casino  brawl which culminates in the villain  leaving The Dark Knight with a mystery  to solve a small strange box that must  be figured out or people are gonna get  hurt the bulk of this episode is spent  unraveling the mysteries of the box and  then dealing with the fallout but the  search for answers has a devastating  impact on the people around Batman kalto  isn't wasting any time as episode 1 has  a few harrowing moments and tough  decisions by the time the episode has  wrapped up the Riddler has made a  permanent mark on the life of Bruce  Wayne and  Batman watch help commissioner for all  his preaching Batman knows when to  sacrifice a pawn to win the game the  means by which players drive the  narrative in season two hasn't changed  drastically from the previous season's  investigations still involve finding  Clues by moving around environments and  interacting with specific objects and  puzzle solving is similarly rudimentary  while the first game the investigation  sequences weren't much more complicated  they at least had a multitude of  variables to think about in this episode  however the solutions were obvious and  the steps to solving conundrums were  simple and it much feel like the world's  greatest detective that's a match  episode 1 as a whole feels more weighted  towards action set-pieces  and although their interactions are  still a series of QuickTime events  there's more involvement in the  moment-to-moment scuffles now after  delivering a flurry of bone-crunching  hooks and uppercuts you can choose  whether you want to follow up with a  roundhouse or a body slam or you'll have  to tap buttons multiple times to ensure  your batarangs hit all the goons in the  area it's a small change but one that  encourages players to stay focused and  engaged as with all telltale games the  way you respond to characters through  dialogue choices not only defines how  the story branches but now also  redefines relationships it can be  difficult to see someone you once  considered a friend now doubting you  through no fault of your own  this shake-up injects further drama into  existing dynamics which means you can  never truly feel at ease during a  conversation while the episode is less  overt than season 1 about trying to  deconstruct Batman and Bruce Wayne the  Riddler is used to assess the principles  they operate under each new saw like  deathtrap questions what sacrifices the  Caped Crusader is willing to make in  pursuit of his justice it's subtle but  telltale is picking at the line between  hero and criminal in a way that could  unravel Batman's sense of self with  interesting implications the Riddler's  arrival has also attracted the attention  of the agency this alpha is headed up by  Amanda Waller and DC fans will  immediately appreciate the implications  her introduction has Waller is known to  be one of the most powerful non  superhuman characters in the DC Universe  regularly scheming and politicking to  keep the worst criminals and greatest  heroes under her thumb she begins to  undermine the relationship between  Commissioner Gordon and Batman by  creating a rift between them something  which the player must then work to mend  she becomes yet another plate for  players to spin a new source of stress  in the game  decision-making moments of course  there's also the matter of John Doe who  abruptly reappears having been released  from Arkham Asylum with a full clean  bill of health however he still ever so  slightly unhinged but at the same time  unusually helpful to Batman's  investigations which once again  emphasizes the actions have consequences  mantra that Telltale's games are built  on  do you accept help from someone who deep  down you know will eventually turn on  you gosh John I don't know my schedule  just so packed these days please don't  blow me off without the need to retell  the origin story and unburdened by that  baggage season 2 of tell towns Batman  series has started strongly with a  variety of well realized new characters  there are a number of tough  decision-making moments that will give  players pause and invite them to  consider the impact their choices could  have while gameplay is not markedly  different combat is tweaked just enough  to make it an improvement over its  previous iteration overall the Enigma is  a successful opening chapter to a Batman  story that's shaping up to be more  ambitious than anything telltale  attempted in the first season  [Music]
 

